During an interview with NBC's "Meet the Press" program, Akbari stated that Iran is now ready for a "doomsday war" with the US, but remains open to diplomatic avenues to avoid missing any opportunities for peace. He emphasized that Iran is willing to engage in negotiations and is prepared to face any challenges, including a "doomsday war," to resist aggression.

However, Akbari also stated that Iran is still prepared to use diplomatic means to resolve the issue. "We are not ready to give up on diplomacy, but we are trying to take advantage of any remaining opportunities for peace," he said.

On September 25, Akbari announced that Iran had submitted a proposal to reopen the Hormuz Strait through Qatar to the US. If the US takes necessary actions based on this proposal, the Hormuz Strait can be reopened in seven days, restoring normal shipping. However, US media reported that Trump rejected the proposal and claimed that Iran is eager to reach an agreement because they have suffered too much.

Akbari clarified that Iran has not yet received a formal rejection of the proposal from the US through the mediation channels. He reiterated that Iran is willing to reopen the Strait as part of a peace agreement. As a condition, the US must first meet Iran's demands, including unfreezing illegally frozen funds and assets, and allowing Iran to sell oil.

Meanwhile, the Revolutionary Guard claimed on September 27 that Iran had detained a US Navy unmanned underwater vehicle (UUV) in the Strait of Hormuz. The claim was reported by Tasnim News Agency on Telegram, stating that the vehicle was a Remus 600 and has been handed over to experts for data extraction. The US Navy did not immediately respond to the request for comment.

On September 25, Akbari announced that Iran had submitted a proposal to reopen the Hormuz Strait through Qatar to the US. If the US takes necessary actions based on this proposal, the Hormuz Strait can be reopened in seven days, restoring normal shipping. However, US media reported that Trump rejected the proposal and claimed that Iran is eager to reach an agreement because they have suffered too much.

Akbari also criticized the US for potentially launching attacks against Iran, stating that Iran's armed forces are now fully prepared to fight. He emphasized that negotiations are the only way to break the deadlock, and Iran insists that the US must meet its conditions, including unfreezing illegally frozen funds and assets, and allowing Iran to sell oil.
