2026-09-29 18:05:19 - agent_core.graph - DEBUG - script attempt 6 content: According to a report by NBC News on September 27, Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi stated that Iran is now prepared for a potential war with the United States after President Donald Trump publicly rejected Iran's proposal for reopening the Strait of Hormuz. However, Araghchi has not given up on diplomatic efforts.

During an interview on NBC's "Meet the Press," Araghchi addressed reports that the US might resume bombing Iran after the midterm elections. He said, "We are now ready for a war, but we are not giving up on diplomacy. We are still trying to find a peaceful solution."

Araghchi emphasized that Iran is willing to engage diplomatically, but if attacked, it will be prepared to fight. He stated, "We are ready for any challenge, including a 'doomsday war,' but we will not miss any opportunity for peace."

Iran's Revolutionary Guard Corps claimed to have detained a US Navy unmanned underwater vehicle (UUV) in the Strait of Hormuz. The claim was made by the Tassnim News Agency on Telegram, which stated the vessel was a Remus 600. Iran has not yet received a formal rejection of its proposal from the US.

Araghchi had previously announced on September 25 that Iran had conveyed a proposal to reopen the Hormuz Strait to the US through Qatar. He said, "If the US takes the necessary actions, the Strait can be reopened within seven days, restoring normal shipping."

However, Araghchi criticized US President Donald Trump's refusal of the proposal, stating that Trump might resume bombing Iran after the midterm elections. He also noted that the US State Department's representative, Michael Waltz, had criticized Iran's proposal as a "fake gesture" that the US could not accept.

Araghchi reiterated that Iran's proposal was part of a peace agreement and that it was willing to reopen the Strait. In exchange, Iran demanded the release of frozen Iranian funds and assets, as well as the ability to sell oil.

Meanwhile, Iran's military spokesman, Amir Hossein Qasemzadeh, stated that Iran's armed forces are prepared to fight against any potential US attacks.
