2026-10-05 23:26:45 - agent_core.graph - DEBUG - script attempt 1 content: According to NBC, Iran's Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi stated on September 27 that Iran is now prepared for a potential war with the US after President Trump publicly rejected Iran's proposal for the Hormuz Strait. However, Iran has not given up on diplomatic efforts.

During an interview with NBC's Meet the Press, Araghchi said, "We are now ready for a war, but we are still trying to find a diplomatic solution." He emphasized that if Iran is attacked again, it will be determined to respond, "even if it means a war of annihilation."

Nevertheless, Araghchi also stated that Iran is still willing to resolve the issue through diplomacy, "now it is up to President Trump to make the choice."

Last week, Iran submitted a proposal to the US via Qatar, suggesting reopening the Hormuz Strait within seven days to restore normal shipping in the global key oil transportation route. Trump rejected the proposal on September 26, stating that Iran is eager to reach an agreement because they have lost too much.

Araghchi clarified that Iran is not proposing new conditions but rather conditions previously promised by the US. In June, the US and Iran signed a memorandum of understanding with temporary ceasefire arrangements, but the agreement broke down after both sides resumed attacks.

Araghchi also mentioned that Iran is not concerned about the US midterm elections but rather about its national interests. Iran's Revolutionary Guard claimed on September 27 that they had detained a US Navy underwater unmanned vehicle (UUV) in the Hormuz Strait. The Revolutionary Guard claimed it was a Remus 600 and has handed it over to experts for data extraction. The US Navy did not immediately respond to the request for comment.

On September 25, Araghchi announced Iran's proposal to reopen the Hormuz Strait through Qatar, suggesting that if the US takes necessary actions, the Strait could be reopened within seven days, restoring normal shipping. However, The Wall Street Journal reported on September 25 that Trump rejected Iran's proposal and told his aides that he might resume bombing on Iran in November, the US midterm elections.

Araghchi stated that the negotiation channel has not yet conveyed the US's formal rejection to Tehran. He insisted that Iran is willing to reopen the Strait as part of a resolution, but as a condition, the US must first meet Iran's demands, including the release of frozen Iranian funds and assets, and the ability to sell oil.

US Ambassador to the UN, Michael Waltz, criticized Iran's latest proposal as a "false posture" that Iran knows the US cannot accept. He said Trump will retain all options.

Regarding potential US attacks on Iran, Iran's military spokesperson, Ali Akramian, stated on September 27 that Iran's armed forces are prepared to fight with all their might.
