2026-10-07 17:13:25 - agent_core.graph - DEBUG - script attempt 2 content: According to a report by NBC News on September 27, Iran's Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi stated that Iran is now prepared for a potential war with the United States after President Trump publicly rejected Iran's proposal for the Hormuz Strait. However, Iran has not given up on diplomatic efforts.

During an interview on NBC's "Meet the Press," Araghchi said, "We are now ready for a war, but we are still trying to pursue diplomacy to avoid missing any opportunity for peace." He emphasized that Iran is willing to negotiate, but will not miss any chance for peace.

Araghchi also noted that Iran is still open to diplomatic solutions, but the United States has not formally rejected Iran's proposal. He reiterated that Iran is willing to reopen the Hormuz Strait as part of a peace agreement, but in exchange, the United States must release frozen Iranian funds and assets, and allow Iran to sell oil.

Araghchi further stated that these are not new demands but commitments made by the United States previously. In June, the United States signed a memorandum of understanding with Iran, but the agreement only lasted a few days before both sides resumed their attacks.

Regarding Trump's claim that Iran is waiting for the results of the upcoming US midterm elections, Araghchi responded, "We are not concerned with the midterm elections, we are concerned with our national interests."

On September 26, Trump had rejected Iran's proposal, stating that Iran is eager to reach an agreement because they have lost too much. Trump also claimed that Iran is using the proposal as a tactic to delay the situation.

In a separate statement, the Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps claimed that they had detained a US Navy unmanned underwater vehicle (UUV) in the Hormuz Strait. The claim was made by the Tassnim News Agency, which cited the Revolutionary Guard Corps. The US Navy did not immediately respond to a request for comment.

Araghchi also announced on September 25 that Iran had submitted a proposal to reopen the Hormuz Strait through Qatar. If the United States took necessary actions, the Strait could be reopened in seven days, allowing normal shipping. However, the Wall Street Journal reported that Trump rejected Iran's proposal and told his aides that he might resume bombing Iran after the midterm elections.

Araghchi clarified that the negotiation team had not yet formally rejected Iran's proposal to the Iranian government. He stated that only negotiations can break the deadlock; as for reopening the Hormuz Strait, Iran insists that the United States must first meet its conditions. Araghchi reiterated that as part of a peace agreement, Iran is willing to reopen the Strait. As an exchange, Iran demands the release of frozen funds and assets, and the ability to sell oil.

US Ambassador to the UN, Michael Waltz, criticized Iran's latest proposal as a "fake attitude" that the US cannot accept. He said that Trump will retain all options.

