During an interview on NBC's "Meet the Press," Araghchi was asked about reports that the US might resume bombing Iran after the midterm elections. He responded, "We are ready for a war, but we are not giving up on diplomacy."

Araghchi emphasized that Iran would not hesitate to defend itself if attacked, "even if it means a war of annihilation." He also stated that Iran remains open to diplomatic solutions, "despite Trump's public stance."

Last week, Iran submitted a proposal to the US through Qatar, suggesting reopening the Strait of Hormuz within seven days to restore normal shipping. US President Donald Trump rejected the proposal on September 26, citing Iran's eagerness to reach an agreement as a result of "losing too badly."

Araghchi clarified that Iran has not yet received a formal rejection of the proposal from the US. He reiterated that Iran is willing to reopen the Strait of Hormuz as part of a deal, but the US must first meet Iran's conditions. These conditions include unfreezing Iranian assets and allowing Iran to sell oil.

Meanwhile, the Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps claimed to have detained a US Navy unmanned underwater vehicle (UUV) in the Strait of Hormuz. The claim was made by the Fars News Agency, citing the Revolutionary Guard Corps. The US Navy did not immediately respond to a request for comment.

Araghchi also mentioned that the US Ambassador to the UN, Michael Waltz, criticized Iran's latest proposal as a "fake gesture" that Iran knew the US would reject. Waltz stated that Trump would retain all options.

In response to potential US attacks, the Iranian military spokesman, Amir Ali Hajdari, stated that the armed forces are prepared and will do their utmost to counter any aggression.
