2026-10-08 17:51:07 - agent_core.graph - DEBUG - script attempt 1 content: A health care company, Nolla Health, based in New York City, announced Monday a pilot program in Utah allowing artificial intelligence to issue initial prescriptions, including acne treatment. The program, detailed in a blog post, is the first in the U. S. authorized by a state regulator to allow AI to issue initial prescriptions. Utah residents 18 and older can download the Nolla Derm application at a discounted rate of $4. 99 per month. After verifying identity, providing informed consent, and completing an intake questionnaire, patients undergo a "quick five-angle face scan" which generates a personalized treatment plan and, if appropriate, an initial prescription in about 10-15 minutes. The pilot will roll out in three stages with physician involvement in each. In the first stage, two licensed physicians independently review and approve every AI-generated prescription. In the second stage, Nolla Derm issues prescriptions directly, with a physician reviewing every case weekly. In the third stage, a 10% sample of prescriptions each month will be reviewed by a physician, and every case involving an escalation or side effect will be reviewed. The company said stages one and two would last at least four and eight weeks, respectively. The pilot requires meeting safety targets, including 95% agreement with physicians and zero serious adverse events, and written approval from the state. Patients can message a licensed physician through the app at no extra cost. The company highlighted acne treatment as a viable starting point due to its well-understood nature, low risk, and strong clinical evidence.
