2026-10-08 18:00:28 - agent_core.graph - DEBUG - script attempt 1 content: A health care company, Nolla Health, based in New York City, announced a pilot program in Utah allowing artificial intelligence to issue initial prescriptions, including acne treatment. The program, detailed in a blog post, is the first in the US authorized by a state regulator to allow AI to issue initial prescriptions. Nolla Health said the pilot aims to close the healthcare access gap by avoiding appointment wait times and costs associated with visiting a dermatologist. The Nolla Derm application will prescribe effective acne treatment for $5 a month, requiring no office visit. Utah residents 18 and older can download the app at a discounted rate of $4.99 per month after verifying their identity, providing informed consent, and completing an intake questionnaire. A quick five-angle face scan generates a personalized treatment plan and an initial prescription in about 10-15 minutes. The pilot will roll out in three stages with physician involvement in each. In the first stage, two licensed physicians independently review and approve every AI-generated prescription. In the second stage, Nolla Derm issues prescriptions directly, with a physician reviewing every case weekly. In the third stage, a 10% sample of prescriptions each month will be reviewed by a physician, and every case involving an escalation or side effect will be reviewed. The post also stated that patients can message a licensed physician through the app at no extra cost. Nolla Health highlighted acne treatment as a viable starting point for the technology due to its well-understood nature, low risk, and strong clinical evidence.
